Here's a live model: set the train's speed in m/s and the distance it covers in 8 seconds updates instantly.

```openui lifecycle=ephemeral id=toy-train-distance

root = LearningSurface([sim], "Toy train distance", "Change the speed; read the distance covered in 8 seconds.", "ephemeral")
sim = Simulation("toy-train-distance", "Toy train on a straight track", [
  { id: "speed", label: "Speed", unit: "m/s", min: 0, max: 30, step: 0.5, value: 6 },
  { id: "time", label: "Time running", unit: "s", min: 1, max: 60, step: 1, value: 8 }
], [
  { id: "distance", label: "Distance travelled", unit: "m", expr: "speed * time", precision: 1, emphasis: true },
  { id: "distance_km", label: "Distance travelled", unit: "km", expr: "(speed * time) / 1000", precision: 3 },
  { id: "kmh", label: "Speed", unit: "km/h", expr: "speed * 3.6", precision: 1 }
], "ephemeral", "Drag the speed slider (or change the running time) and the distance updates at once. Try a slow pass and a fast pass: the distance scales directly with speed for the same 8 seconds.")
```

The distance readout is the emphasised one — it opens plotted against speed, with the 8-second time held as a second parameter you can also change if you want to test a different run length.

Here's a live model of your run — set the speed and the distance updates instantly.

```openui lifecycle=ephemeral id=toy-train-speed-distance

root = LearningSurface([sim], "Toy train: speed → distance", "Distance travelled over a fixed 8-second run.", "ephemeral")
sim = Simulation("train-sim", "Toy train on a 8-second run", [
  { id: "speed", label: "Train speed", unit: "m/s", min: 0, max: 20, step: 0.5, value: 4 }
], [
  { id: "distance", label: "Distance travelled", unit: "m", expr: "speed * 8", precision: 1, emphasis: true }
], "ephemeral", "The train runs for a fixed 8 seconds, so distance is just speed × 8. Move the speed slider and watch the distance readout change. Try to find the speed that puts it exactly 40 m away, then check a speed near zero and a fast one to see the pattern.")
```

The rule the model is built on: **distance = speed × time**, and here time is pinned at 8 seconds — so the readout is your speed multiplied by 8, in metres.

Two things worth noticing as you drag: the readout is perfectly linear (double the speed, double the distance), and at 0 m/s the distance is 0 m because the train never leaves the start line.

Press **Share what I found** when you've settled on settings, and I'll read the actual numbers back to you.

Straight to it: distance is just **d = v × t**, and with t fixed at 8 s, the whole thing is linear — double the speed, double the distance. Here's the control so you can see it move:

```openui lifecycle=ephemeral id=toy-train-speed-distance

root = LearningSurface([note, sim], "Toy train: speed → distance in 8 seconds", "Drag the speed slider and watch the distance update instantly.", "ephemeral")
note = Explanation("A toy train runs for a fixed **8 seconds** on a straight track. The distance it covers is **d = v × t**, so with t locked at 8 s, distance is just 8 times the speed. Try moving the speed slider and watch the two readouts change together — they scale in a fixed proportion, with no surprises.")
sim = Simulation("train-speed-sim", "Toy train speed and distance", [
  { id: "speed", label: "Train speed", unit: "m/s", min: 0, max: 30, step: 0.5, value: 5 },
  { id: "duration", label: "Run duration", unit: "s", min: 1, max: 30, step: 1, value: 8 }
], [
  { id: "distance", label: "Distance travelled", unit: "m", expr: "speed * duration", precision: 1, emphasis: true },
  { id: "halfway", label: "Distance at 4 s", unit: "m", expr: "speed * 4", precision: 1 }
], "ephemeral", "Set the speed, then read the total distance. The duration slider stays at 8 s for your main test — move it afterwards to see what changes when the run time changes.")
```

The **Distance travelled** readout is the emphasised one, plotted against your speed slider, so you can see the straight-line relationship immediately. When you've played with it, tell me what you noticed — e.g. whether anything about the graph surprised you.
